  "briefing": {
    "headline": "The simulation predicts two home days a week would cut leavers over the next 12 months by about a third, from 632 to 445 (-32%, roughly 187 fewer departures out of 5,000), with employees feeling clearly positive (+1.00 on a -2 to +2 scale).",
    "most_at_risk": "Caregivers (-38%), long commuters (-36%) and women (-37%) gain the most because the two days give back hours of commuting and family time, while short commuters gain least (-28%) and describe it as 'a nice bonus' that doesn't change their plans.",
    "risks": [
      "Some of the gain may be temporary: several people who benefit most say it 'won't make me stay forever' or that they're 'still going to look around', so this eases pressure to leave rather than removing it.",
      "People will watch closely to see whether working from home quietly hurts reviews or promotions despite the stated policy; if they come to believe it does, staff may stop using the days or lose trust, and much of the retention gain could disappear.",
      "Managers' own leaving falls slightly more (-34%) in the simulation, but day-to-day they control whether home days are respected; inconsistent treatment across teams could turn a well-received policy into a grievance."
    ],
    "design_changes": [
      "Make Wednesday and Friday home days a standard entitlement rather than something managers grant case by case, and brief managers before launch on how to run hybrid teams fairly.",
      "Back the 'promotion process does not change' promise with evidence: track reviews and promotions for people who use home days versus those who don't, and share the results with staff after the first cycle.",
      "Run it as a trial with a clear measurement plan, for example comparing leaving rates, performance and promotions between groups or over time, so you can confirm the effect with real data before treating it as permanent and before considering further changes such as more home days."
    ],
    "confidence": "medium, the direction is consistent across every group and every sampled voice, but the numbers come from a synthetic model built on only 48 modelled voices across 24 groups, so treat -32% as a sign of a meaningful reduction rather than a precise forecast."
  },
